From a8a188a3806e372a0986d5ad9ccc635f957e115d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: primez <mpincoski@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 07:36:13 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 32/41] [mpegts] Read the size and duration of each audio frame
 from the frame header

The TS audio parsers advance the frame clock by a fixed frame duration,
and some read a wrong frame size:

- MPEG-2 and MPEG-2.5 Layer III frames hold 576 samples, and the frame
  size uses 72 instead of 144. The parser joined each two frames into
  one sample with the wrong timestamps. Layer I frames hold 384 samples.
  A mono stream had 2 channels (channel_mode 3 was compared with 11).
- An E-AC-3 frame holds 1, 2, 3 or 6 blocks of 256 samples. A dependent
  frame (7.1 channels) extends the frame before at the same time, so
  the dependent frame now joins the frame before in one sample, as in
  FFmpeg and the packed audio reader. A dependent frame without the frame before is dropped.
- An ADTS frame holds 1 to 4 raw data blocks, and a LATM frame 1 to 64
  sub frames, each of 1024 samples. The packed audio reader also used
  1024 samples for each ADTS frame.
- An ADTS frame length equal to the header, or a LATM length of 0,
  gave an empty frame that moved the audio clock. An ADTS header with
  the sample rate index 13 to 15, as a sync word in the frame data, is
  no header.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
